This tool represents my first effort at reverse engineering family history
databases from the BK5 program. You must have all of the relevant files
within the current directory in order to run the code.

Build with `make` from the repository root. The most useful current commands
are:

    ./dlbk5dump --dump-id-records
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-marriages
    ./dlbk5dump --family 0x9a
    ./tools/bk5-altkeys.py
    ./tools/bk5-mail.py
    ./tools/bk5-options.py
    make gedcom > indexed.ged
    make gedcom-file
    make audit
    ./tools/bk5-ancestry.py indexed.ged --person 3

The person dump decodes the Btrieve key-0 index, then scans physical fixed
record slots for valid person numbers missing from that index. Raw-page scans
remain important because old or orphaned records may use a different view of
the variable data; no GEDCOM export is considered complete until both sources
have been reconciled. The current GEDCOM command also includes raw-only
records under synthetic identifiers, so it is an intermediate extraction
rather than the final GEDCOM.

The exporter preserves all five location and source slot numbers from fixed
person records and resolves them from `BKLOCATE.DT5` and `BKSOURCE.DT5` as
audit notes. The empirically validated date-slot order is BIRT, BAPM, DEAT,
BURI, and other; the first four slots receive matching `PLAC` values when
present. Raw slot notes remain so the event mapping can be audited.

The ancestry audit follows only explicit `FAMC` links in that export. It is
useful for separating the oldest linked ancestor from older, disconnected
records and prints the BK5 family identifiers and marriage dates used by each
link. Add `--terminals` to list the linked ancestors for whom no further
parent-family record is present.

Dates found in raw variable-page person records are retained as
`BK5 raw date at ...` notes when their event type cannot be proven from the
fixed-record layout.

Marriage records without an explicit family ID are linked only when both
spouse records independently contain the same family reference. Other
ambiguous marriage scans are retained as header notes with their raw spouse
references and dates.

Some older BKMARR entries use a compact key immediately before the date
instead of the normal spouse-pair header. The decoder preserves these as
layout candidates and may attach a date only when the compact person record
has one unambiguous FAMS reference; such links are labelled as inferred.

`ALTKEYS.DT5` is also inspected as fixed 162-byte slots. Its one non-empty
slot is retained as an unassigned GEDCOM header note because the file contains
no person identifier tying it to an individual.

Printable text in `BKMAIL.DT5` is preserved as offset-labelled, unassigned
GEDCOM header notes because the address records contain no person IDs.

`bk5-options.py` compares the active and backup option files. These files are
configuration metadata, not person records, so they are audited separately
and are not turned into GEDCOM individuals or relationships.

Decoded `BKSOURCE.DT5` descriptions are also emitted as standalone GEDCOM
`SOUR` records. Person source-slot notes remain in place because the exact
event citation for each legacy slot is not inferred.

`make audit` regenerates the intermediate GEDCOM and checks decoded date
coverage, family-reference coverage, duplicate IDs, and dangling links.
`make gedcom-file` writes the same export to `indexed.ged`.

`--dump-relations` is diagnostic only: its pointer hits are unvalidated
B-tree/index candidates and are not used as genealogy relationships.
